-A proof-based real analysis collection, built from a construction of the real
-numbers rather than from axioms taken on trust. Cauchy sequences of rationals
-and Dedekind cuts each produce a complete ordered field, and from the least
-upper bound property follow suprema and infima, roots and rational powers,
-countability, convergent sequences, upper and lower limits, series and the tests
-that settle them, and the open, closed, compact and connected subsets of the
-line. Limits and continuity come next, with the intermediate and extreme value
-theorems and uniform continuity, then the derivative and the mean value
-theorems, Darboux, L'Hopital, Taylor and convexity, then the Riemann integral,
-its criterion, the fundamental theorem of calculus, improper integrals, bounded
-variation and Riemann-Stieltjes. Uniform convergence repairs what pointwise
-convergence loses, and carries Arzela-Ascoli, Stone-Weierstrass and power
-series. The exponential, the logarithm, the sine and the cosine are defined by
-their series, so pi is a theorem rather than a picture. Calculus in several
-variables closes the collection, with the total derivative, the inverse and
-implicit function theorems, Jordan content, Fubini, change of variables, arc
-length and line integrals.
+The Real Analysis pages develop the real line and calculus from completeness. They compare Cauchy-sequence and Dedekind-cut constructions, establish order and topological properties of the real line, and build the theory of sequences, limits, series, continuity, and differentiation. Integration proceeds through the Riemann and gauge integrals, the fundamental theorems, improper integrals, and bounded variation. Further pages treat uniform convergence and power series, define the exponential, logarithm, and trigonometric functions, and establish results on multivariable differentiation, inverse and implicit maps, Jordan content, change of variables, line and surface integrals, and classical integral theorems. The collection also covers ordinary differential equations, convexity, and real special functions.
 
-The rest of the library leans on it heavily. Measure theory takes over where the
-Riemann integral stops, functional analysis starts from the normed spaces and
-completeness proved here, complex analysis reuses the power series and the
-rectifiable path, metric topology generalises the sequence and supremum
-arguments, and differential geometry consumes the total derivative, the inverse
-and implicit function theorems, Jordan content and the change of variables
-formula.
+Set Theory, Topology, Linear Algebra, Combinatorics, and Abstract Algebra support arguments throughout these pages. Measure Theory and Probability draw on its integration results; Functional Analysis, Complex Analysis, Fourier Analysis, and Partial Differential Equations use completeness, limits, and series; Differential Geometry relies on multivariable calculus and change of variables.
